 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "They may be describing **two different measures of air quality**, rather than a contradiction. If you mean Singapore’s PSI, its “moderate” reading reflects pollution averaged over **24 hours**. An app showing “unhealthy” may be using a more current AQI reading, a different averaging method, or a different monitoring location—so a recent spike can show up in the app before it has much effect on the 24-hour PSI. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?utm_source=openai))\n\nFor a decision **right now**, check the **1-hour PM2.5 reading** alongside the PSI. Singapore’s NEA recommends that reading for immediate activities, such as whether to go for a jog. Also check whether your app says **“unhealthy”** or **“unhealthy for sensitive groups”**—those are different categories. ([haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg/who-we-are?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you tell me the app, your location, and the two readings, I can help pinpoint the difference.",
